  <h2 id="ca">CA without maths, honestly</h2>

  <p>
    CA is the question asked most often, so it gets its own honest look. Any stream can register, and you can start after Class 10, although the Foundation exam itself comes after Class 12.
  </p>

  <p>
    Foundation Paper 3 is Quantitative Aptitude: Business Mathematics, Statistics and Logical Reasoning. Prep sources report it as 100 multiple-choice questions in two hours, with 40, 40 and 20 marks respectively, and topics that run through logarithms, mathematics of finance and basic calculus. That is more than Class 10 arithmetic. ICAI's own FAQ says you pass the Foundation with at least 40 per cent in each paper and 50 per cent in aggregate, at one sitting, with 0.25 negative marking on wrong answers in the objective papers. Confirm current rules at <a href="https://www.icai.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">icai.org</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Multi-stage competition:</strong> Foundation is only the first filter. Intermediate, Final and articleship follow, each with its own pass rate and time cost. Reported Foundation pass rates were about 14.8 per cent (September 2025), 19.2 per cent (January 2026) and 20.1 per cent (May 2026). These figures are not split by maths background, so they do not show that non-maths students fail more.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Add the three sittings and about 2.99 lakh candidates appeared, and about 53,800 passed. That is roughly 18 per cent per attempt, and the CA Foundation runs three times a year, so one bad sitting does not end the year.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The route around Paper 3:</strong> ICAI's FAQ lets commerce graduates with 55% marks, and other graduates with 60%, register directly for Intermediate without the Foundation. A B.Com plus a strong skill stack keeps that door open, so Paper 3 is a choice, not a wall.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A student without maths usually finds Paper 3 harder than the accounting papers, and later stages bring costing and finance sums. It is manageable with practice, but choose CA because the work suits you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The upside is real: a practising CA can own a firm, set fees and hire staff. That is a scalable ownership route, and it comes after a long climb.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you want to compare CA with the other finance credentials, read <a href="/blog/career-options/ca-vs-cma-vs-cfa-india-which-is-better/">CA vs CMA vs CFA</a>. If CA is not for you, <a href="/blog/career-options/career-after-bcom-other-than-ca-and-mba/">careers after BCom other than CA and MBA</a> maps the alternatives.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quant-floor">Open, but with a quant section waiting</h2>

  <p>
    CA Foundation, bank PO and IPMAT do not ask what subjects you took. They ask you to perform on a timed quantitative section. A bank PO prelim gives you 20 minutes for 30 quant questions, which is 40 seconds each, so speed matters as much as method. Treat that as a training problem.
  </p>

