  <h2 id="cs">Company Secretary: the lightest quant route into a professional qualification</h2>

  <p>
    ICSI restructured its entrance test, CSEET, from 2026. It now has four papers of 100 marks each: Business Communication, Fundamentals of Accounting, Economic and Business Environment, and Business Laws and Management. None of them is a maths or quantitative aptitude paper.
  </p>

  <figure class="article-visual">
    <img src="/images/blog/career-options/career-options-in-commerce-without-maths/career-options-in-commerce-without-maths-linear-company-secretary-lightest-quant.webp" alt="Process panel showing the four CSEET papers and the next checks for the Company Secretary route" title="Company Secretary: the lightest quant route into a professional qualification" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>ICSI restructured CSEET from 2026; none of its four papers is a maths or quantitative aptitude paper.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    To sit the test you must be an Indian national who has passed or is appearing in Class 12. ICSI's syllabus sets no stream. Reported pass rules are 40 per cent in each paper and 50 per cent overall, with three sessions a year and offline exams.
  </p>

  <p>
    Graduates, final-year bachelor's students and passers of ICSI Foundation, CA Final or CMA Final are reported as exempt, and can register straight for CS Executive. Fees are reported as ₹7,500 registration plus ₹1,500 a sitting. Check <a href="https://www.icsi.edu/students/restructured-cseet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">icsi.edu</a> before you pay, because these are reported figures.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Multi-stage competition:</strong> CSEET is a light gate, but Executive and Professional stages follow, with accounting, tax and company-law papers that carry real numerical work. Choose CS for the legal and compliance work, not because the first exam looked easy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="skill-first">Skill-first commerce paths that never ask about maths</h2>

  <p>
    These paths are ordered by real growth headroom, not by prestige. Digital marketing and sales lead because pay follows results and freelance or ownership routes exist. Accounting and GST practice follow because a small practice can scale. HR and operations climb steadily. Law is a formal track with its own exam.
  </p>

